Add todo_write: a plan the model keeps for itself

A long task drifts when the plan lives only in the conversation: compaction trims
it, the sliding window drops it, and the model re-derives a slightly different
plan every turn. todo_write keeps a per-session list and, crucially, the list is
**injected into the system prompt** rather than stored in history — a list that
survives compaction is worth more than one that survives nothing.

Only planning, never execution: the list changes no permission, blocks no call,
and is not persisted. Anything that must outlive the process belongs to
save_note or schedule, not to this table.

Three hard limits, all borrowed from implementations that learned them the hard
way: whole-table replace (per-item editing leaves "who deleted item 3" with no
answer), one in_progress at a time (deepseek-harness makes this a required
deployment choice and we take the strict branch), and caps of 20 items and 4000
characters each (hermes' numbers). A rejected write changes nothing at all —
half a list is worse than none.

One tool does both jobs: passing todos writes, omitting them reads back the
authoritative list, so the model never has to guess what it wrote.

Subagents get neither this tool nor delegate_task: the list belongs to the
parent's task line, and a child writing it would silently overwrite the plan it
was sent to execute.

7 tool tests (the three limits, whole-table replace, revision, read-back,
per-session isolation, a rejected write leaving the old list intact, the stable
injection header) and 2 app-server tests (the list appears in the next turn's
system prompt; a session with no list gets no section) plus 2 evals. Docs:
README tool row, limitations, internals 6, CHANGELOG — EN + zh.
